L.append(lesson(
 "technology-breakthroughs","Technology breakthroughs","Stronger spin scattering tracks a higher superconducting temperature",
 "Understand the new correlation that supports spin-fluctuation pairing in electron-doped cuprates—and its limits.",
 "New research · Nature Physics · October 2026",
 '''<p>Ordinary superconductors pair electrons through vibrations of the crystal lattice. Copper-oxide superconductors work at higher temperatures, but the pairing “glue” has been uncertain.</p><p>A 2026 study measured one smooth electron-doped cuprate film whose critical temperature changed along its length. High magnetic fields exposed the normal-state resistance. The team reported that stronger scattering from fluctuating electron spins tracked higher critical temperature, supporting spin-fluctuation-mediated pairing on the electron-doped side.</p>''',
 '''<p>Think of six contacts placed along one film. Disorder stays nearly constant, while cerium doping and <code>Tc</code> vary smoothly. At each contact, the researchers extract a scattering strength from resistance measurements. The two quantities rise together. This controlled gradient makes the comparison stronger than comparing unrelated samples.</p>''',
 {"when":"Use this as a research deep dive when evaluating how carefully designed gradients and high-field measurements can isolate a mechanism in quantum materials.","example":"A materials team asks whether one candidate interaction changes systematically with the measured transition temperature across a single fabricated film.","decision":"Treat the finding as evidence for electron-doped cuprates, not a recipe for room-temperature superconductors. Reproduction, theory, other compounds, and the higher-Tc hole-doped side remain necessary."},
 "Change the synthetic correlation and inspect whether scattering and Tc rise together. Then separate correlation evidence from a complete mechanism claim.",
 "The browser draws invented teaching values, not the paper's data or fit. It omits sample fabrication, field strength, uncertainty, resistance analysis, competing mechanisms, and hole-doped materials. The research claim should be assessed in the original paper.",
 '''<div id="world"><label>Spin-scattering correlation <input id="correlation" type="range" min="0" max="10" value="8"></label><div id="corrWorld" class="world" aria-live="polite"></div></div>''',
 "function paintCorr(){const c=+correlation.value;let bars='';for(let i=1;i<=6;i++){const h=20+i*c*2;bars+='<div class=bar style=\"height:'+h+'px\"><span>point '+i+'<br>Tc '+Math.round(h/4)+' K</span></div>';}corrWorld.innerHTML='<div class=corr>'+bars+'</div><p><strong>'+c+'/10 coupling in this teaching model.</strong> A rising trend supports a relationship; it does not solve the hole-doped side or prove a universal recipe.</p>';};correlation.oninput=paintCorr;paintCorr();",
 [q("Why use a smooth Tc gradient along one film?",["It compares many states while keeping disorder more controlled","It guarantees room-temperature behavior","It removes the need for measurement"],0,"The gradient helps isolate the changing variable within one sample."),q("What did the reported correlation support?",["Spin-fluctuation-mediated pairing in the electron-doped material","A finished theory for all superconductors","Zero-resistance copper at room temperature"],0,"The claim is important but scoped."),q("What major boundary remains?",["The higher-Tc hole-doped side needs an additional ingredient","No electrons were measured","The sample was a web simulation"],0,"The authors explicitly say only part of the cuprate problem is solved.")],
 [{"code":"scattering_strength = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]","note":"Create six ordered teaching measurements."},{"code":"critical_temperature = [4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19]","note":"Use synthetic temperatures that rise with scattering."},{"code":"paired = list(zip(scattering_strength, critical_temperature))","note":"Pair each contact's two values."},{"code":"trend_is_positive = all(paired[i][1] > paired[i-1][1] for i in range(1, len(paired)))","note":"The teaching data have a positive trend, not a causal proof."}],
 {"scattering_strength":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"critical_temperature":[4,7,10,13,16,19],"paired":[[1,4],[2,7],[3,10],[4,13],[5,16],[6,19]],"trend_is_positive":True},
 [["Evidence for spin-fluctuation-mediated pairing in electron-doped cuprates","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03454-6","Nature Physics; published October 2026"],["University of Bristol research release","https://www.bristol.ac.uk/news/2026/october/researchers-help-solve-mystery-of-high-temperature-superconductivity.html","Published October 20, 2026"]],
 "The rising bars answer what the experiment linked: an interaction strength and a critical temperature across controlled positions—not a generic superconductivity icon.",
 ["Which alternative explanation would you try to rule out when two measured quantities rise together?","What result on hole-doped cuprates would strengthen or weaken the proposed mechanism?"]))

L.append(lesson(
 "ci-cd-github-actions","CI/CD & GitHub Actions","A job must match every runner label",
 "Route work with labels and groups, while keeping scheduling separate from security and isolation.",
 "GitHub Actions · runner routing",
 '''<p>A delivery request that says “refrigerated, electric, Manhattan depot” needs a truck with every listed capability. A truck matching only two conditions is not eligible.</p><p>In GitHub Actions, an array in <code>runs-on</code> is an AND filter: a runner must match every value. A runner group narrows which repositories may target a pool. Labels describe scheduling capability; the group access policy and runner hardening form the stronger boundary.</p>''',
 '''<p>A GPU test requests <code>[self-hosted, linux, x64, gpu]</code>. Runner A has linux+x64 but no gpu, so it waits. Runner B has all four labels and is eligible. If the job also requires group <code>ml-secure</code>, a perfectly labeled runner in another group remains ineligible.</p>''',
 {"when":"Use labels to select OS, architecture, accelerators, size, or installed capabilities. Use runner groups to limit which repositories can access sensitive or expensive runner pools.","example":"Only trusted ML repositories may target a private-network GPU pool; ordinary builds stay on ephemeral GitHub-hosted runners.","decision":"Keep labels few and factual, monitor queue time, and use autoscaling/ephemeral runners where appropriate. Treat self-hosted runners as privileged infrastructure. Groups reduce scope, but they do not make untrusted workflow code safe."},
 "Toggle runner labels and the group. The job becomes eligible only when every label and the required group match.",
 "The browser is a set-intersection model. It does not contact GitHub, inspect runner registration, model scale sets, custom label limits, permissions, repository policy, queue priority, concurrency, compromised runners, or network isolation.",
 '''<div id="world"><label><input id="runnerGpu" type="checkbox"> runner has gpu label</label><label><input id="runnerGroup" type="checkbox" checked> runner belongs to ml-secure group</label><div id="runnerWorld" class="world" aria-live="polite"></div></div>''',
 "function paintRunner(){const labels=runnerGpu.checked,group=runnerGroup.checked,ok=labels&&group;runnerWorld.innerHTML='<div class=runner-grid><div class=\"route ok\"><strong>Job requires</strong><br>self-hosted · linux · x64 · gpu<br>group: ml-secure</div><div class=\"route '+(labels?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>Label filter</strong><br>'+(labels?'all four match':'gpu missing')+'</div><div class=\"route '+(group?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>Group filter</strong><br>'+(group?'ml-secure matches':'wrong group')+'</div></div><p class=\"'+(ok?'ok':'wait')+'\">'+(ok?'ELIGIBLE: job can be scheduled':'QUEUED: no eligible runner')+'</p>';};runnerGpu.onchange=paintRunner;runnerGroup.onchange=paintRunner;paintRunner();",
 [q("A job lists self-hosted, linux, x64, gpu. Which runner is eligible?",["One matching all four labels","One matching any single label","Every organization runner"],0,"Array labels are combined as an AND filter."),q("Why add a runner group?",["To restrict which repositories may access the pool","To turn labels into secrets","To skip workflow permissions"],0,"Groups add an access boundary around runner sets."),q("What do labels not prove?",["That a runner is hardened against untrusted code","That a capability is advertised","That the scheduler can match a value"],0,"Scheduling metadata is not a substitute for isolation and trust policy.")],
 [{"code":"required_labels = ['self-hosted', 'linux', 'x64', 'gpu']","note":"Declare every job capability."},{"code":"runner_labels = ['self-hosted', 'linux', 'x64']","note":"The first runner is missing gpu."},{"code":"eligible_before = all(label in runner_labels for label in required_labels)","note":"The check is false, so the job waits."},{"code":"runner_labels.append('gpu'); eligible_after = all(label in runner_labels for label in required_labels)","note":"Adding the last label makes the runner eligible."}],
 {"required_labels":["self-hosted","linux","x64","gpu"],"runner_labels":["self-hosted","linux","x64","gpu"],"eligible_before":False,"eligible_after":True},
 [["GitHub Docs: choosing the runner for a job","https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/write-workflows/choose-where-workflows-run/choose-the-runner-for-a-job","Living documentation; checked October 24, 2026"],["GitHub Docs: runner groups","https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/runners/runner-groups","Living documentation; checked October 24, 2026"],["GitHub Docs: secure use of self-hosted runners","https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use","Living documentation; checked October 24, 2026"]],
 "The three filters show scheduling as set intersection: job labels, runner labels, and group membership must all align.",
 ["Which runner capabilities in your organization deserve labels, and which deserve separate groups?","What evidence would show a self-hosted runner is ephemeral, patched, and isolated from untrusted repositories?"]))

L.append(lesson(
 "apis-microservices","APIs & Microservices","CORS controls browser sharing; it is not API authorization",
 "Trace preflight and response sharing, then keep authentication and authorization on the server.",
 "Browser APIs · CORS",
 '''<p>A hotel curtain can stop someone in one room from seeing into another. It does not stop a person outside the hotel from calling the front desk. CORS is a browser-enforced sharing rule, not a universal network firewall.</p><p>For some cross-origin requests, the browser first sends an <code>OPTIONS</code> preflight. The server's CORS response says which origins, methods, headers, and credential modes the browser may expose to JavaScript. A curl client or another service is not constrained by the browser's CORS checks.</p>''',
 '''<p><code>app.example</code> wants to call <code>api.example/orders</code> with <code>Authorization</code>. The browser preflights. If the server allows that origin and header, JavaScript may read the response. If not, the browser blocks sharing. The API still must validate the token and verify that this user may read order 42.</p>''',
 {"when":"Configure CORS when browser JavaScript must call an API on another origin. Keep the allowed origin, method, header, and credential policy narrow.","example":"A React operations console on app.example calls an order API on api.example with a bearer token.","decision":"Use server-side authentication and object-level authorization for protection. Use CORS only for browser sharing. Avoid reflecting arbitrary origins, and never combine wildcard origin with credential assumptions. Test simple requests, preflighted requests, and failures."},
 "Choose caller type, origin permission, and token validity. Watch browser sharing and server authorization as separate decisions.",
 "The browser model simplifies the Fetch Standard. Real simple requests may skip preflight; credentials, redirects, caches, exposed headers, opaque responses, service workers, cookies, CSRF, and framework behavior add detail. It makes no network calls.",
 '''<div id="world"><label>Caller <select id="caller"><option value="browser">browser JavaScript</option><option value="service">server-to-server</option></select></label><label><input id="originAllowed" type="checkbox"> origin allowed by CORS</label><label><input id="tokenValid" type="checkbox" checked> valid token and order permission</label><div id="corsWorld" class="world" aria-live="polite"></div></div>''',
 "function paintCors(){const browser=caller.value==='browser',cors=originAllowed.checked,auth=tokenValid.checked;const sent=!browser||cors;const shared=browser?cors&&auth:auth;corsWorld.innerHTML='<div class=browser-grid><div class=\"node '+(browser&&!cors?'fail':'ok')+'\"><strong>'+(browser?'Browser CORS gate':'No browser CORS gate')+'</strong><br>'+(browser?(cors?'preflight permits sharing':'response not shared to JS'):'request goes to API')+'</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=\"response '+(auth?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>API authorization</strong><br>'+(auth?'order 42 allowed':'401/403')+'</div></div><p><strong>'+(shared?'Caller receives allowed data':'Caller does not receive allowed data')+'</strong>. CORS decision and authorization decision remain separate.</p>';};caller.onchange=paintCors;originAllowed.onchange=paintCors;tokenValid.onchange=paintCors;paintCors();",
 [q("CORS denies app.example. What does that establish?",["A conforming browser will not share the response with that JavaScript","The API cannot be called by curl","The user is unauthenticated"],0,"CORS is a browser response-sharing protocol."),q("What must still protect GET /orders/42?",["Server-side authentication and object-level authorization","Access-Control-Allow-Origin alone","A JavaScript variable"],0,"Non-browser clients bypass browser CORS enforcement."),q("Why can a preflight succeed but the API still return 403?",["CORS and authorization answer different questions","OPTIONS logs the user in","403 means the origin is always allowed"],0,"The browser may be allowed to make/read a request whose user lacks business permission.")],
 [{"code":"origin_allowed = True","note":"The API permits browser sharing with app.example."},{"code":"token_valid = True","note":"The caller presents a valid token."},{"code":"order_permission = False","note":"The authenticated subject still lacks access to order 42."},{"code":"browser_may_share = origin_allowed; api_returns = 200 if token_valid and order_permission else 403","note":"CORS passes while authorization correctly denies the object."}],
 {"origin_allowed":True,"token_valid":True,"order_permission":False,"browser_may_share":True,"api_returns":403},
 [["WHATWG Fetch Standard: CORS protocol","https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol","Living standard; checked October 24, 2026"],["WHATWG Fetch Standard: CORS-preflight fetch","https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#cors-preflight-fetch","Living standard; checked October 24, 2026"],["MDN CORS guide","https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS","Living reference; checked October 24, 2026"]],
 "The two gates answer the common misconception directly: browser sharing can pass or fail independently of API authorization.",
 ["Which browser origins, methods, and headers should your most sensitive API actually allow?","How would you prove that curl cannot read order 42 without authorization even though curl ignores CORS?"]))

